{
    "allowed_use": "The finite completion of a staged three-slot LDGSTS operand ring at a measured declared operand-pair panel and CTA grid with one resident CTA per SM, as intercept plus rounds times the per-tier round service, for round counts inside the measured domain.",
    "derived_model": {
        "not_a_production_floor": "These per-round services are valid for this probe's ring only. Shaping the probe's bytes exactly like production -- the whole A panel issued with a zero cp.async source size, only B fetched -- still leaves its marginal round above the cuBLAS ring's: separating production's finite intercept from its marginal round over several K per topology gives 344.6 ns/round at (16384, grid 64), 442.0 at (16384, grid 80) and 557.1 at (24576, grid 64), while this probe's production-shaped minimum is 442.7, 458.7 and 777.3 respectively, i.e. 4 to 39 percent higher. The already published SASS-pinned ring is higher still (608 ns at the first topology). The cause is the consumer: a copy-only ring retires a slot with a volatile shared read and two barriers, while cuBLAS retires it through LDSM and HMMA. No copy-only ring can therefore serve as a production per-round floor, and the byte-capacity route already prices the marginal round correctly (320.6 ns modelled against 344.6 ns measured at the first topology). What transfers from this probe is the zero-fill mechanism itself, which is a property of cp.async: a partially zero-filled declared panel costs the same round as a fully fetched one.",
